abstract: >-
  Code, de-leaked evaluation splits, and benchmark results for a graded leakage audit of
  gene-disease link prediction on a multi-species, Monarch-derived knowledge graph
  (452,012 nodes; 5,860,539 directed edges). Four knowledge-graph-embedding models
  spanning two families (translational: TransE, RotatE; bilinear: DistMult, ComplEx)
  and five topological baselines (Random, Common-Neighbors, Adamic-Adar, Jaccard,
  Preferential-Attachment) are evaluated under one shared ranking harness on 4,228
  held-out human gene-disease test edges across four regimes (R0 random split, R1 direct
  train-test overlap removed, R2 degree-preserving rewiring, R3 cross-species orthology
  removed; seeds 42/1/7), with filtered full ranking against all diseases as the primary
  protocol and 50 sampled negatives as the secondary one. Under full ranking, KGE models
  retain 18-30% of their R0 MRR after degree-preserving rewiring (63-79% against sampled
  negatives), so most performance requires structure beyond degree and sampled ranking
  overstates the role of degree. Direct train-test overlap (R1) lowers KGE MRR by 10-32%
  but overlap-heuristic MRR by at most 2%, and removing cross-species orthology (R3)
  lowers no method's MRR by more than 1.1%. The release includes the de-leaked splits,
  per-regime result JSONs under both protocols, table/figure generators that read only
  the frozen result JSONs, and a one-command regeneration script.
